#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175709 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca77fd-e6ef-568f-be32-e1ae47c3d8dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given One Complex Root"}] :question/id #uuid "b4649e26-6d7b-5c3e-9c8e-d55a9db5eb29" :question/math-academy-id "e-5797" :question/problem "Given that $x=2\\textrm{i}$ is a solution to the quartic equation $x^4 + 13 x^2 + 36 = 0$, find all of the solutions." :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^4 + 13 x^2 + 36\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and\n\n$$\nx_1=2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nis a root of $f(x)$, the complex conjugate\n\n$$\n\\overline{x_1}=\\overline{2\\textrm{i}}=-2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x)$.\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem,\n$\\big(x-2\\text{i}\\big)\\big(x-(-2\\text{i})\\big)$\nis a factor of $f(x)$. Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 2\\textrm{i})(x - (-2\\textrm{i})) &= (x - 2\\textrm{i})(x + 2\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - (2\\textrm{i})^{2}) \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2+4$ using long division.\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 9; x^{2} + 4x^{4} + 13x^{2} + 36; x^{4} + 4x^{2}_(―); 2x^{4} +9x^{2} + 36; 2x^{4} +9x^{2} + 36_(―); 2x^{4} + 2x^{2} +00\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x^2+4)(x^2+9)\n$$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve\n\n$$\nx^2+9 = 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} &= -9 \\\\\nx &= ± 3\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of our quartic equation are\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2\\textrm{i}, \\, \\pm 3 \\textrm{i}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175735 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57987f7d-21e1-51cb-a745-a24f2b257f11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given Two Real Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "d8bf44c3-384b-553a-931f-a09ac2a3a649" :question/math-academy-id "e-8802" :question/problem "Given that $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are solutions to the equation $x^4 - 6 x^3 + 12 x^2 + 6 x - 13 = 0$, find the remaining solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^4 - 6 x^3 + 12 x^2 + 6 x - 13\n$$\n\nSince $x_1=-1$ and $x_2=1$ are roots of $f(x)$, then according to the factor theorem,\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-1) = x^2-1\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x)$.\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2-1$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\nx^{2} - 6x + 13; x^{2} - 1x^{4} - 6x^{3} + 12x^{2} + 6x - 13; x^{4} - x^{2}_(―); - 6x^{3} + 13x^{2} + 6x; - 6x^{3} + 6x_(―); x^{4} + 313x^{2} - 13; x^{4} + 313x^{2} - 13_(―); x^{4} + 3x^{3} - 5x^{2} + 150\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x^2-1)(x^2-6x+13)\n$$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve\n\n$$\nx^2-6x+13=0\n$$\n\nby completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 6x + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 6x + 3^{2}) - 3^{2} + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nx - 3 &= ± 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\nx &= 3 ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=\\pm 1$ and\n\n$$\nx=3\\pm 2\\textrm{i}\n$$"}]}
